1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,060
Since the dawn of time, prophets and warriors have gone to the desert in search of visions.

2
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:19,720
There is ancient magic in the arid isolation that seems to make the most outrageous visions come to life.

3
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,300
Welcome to Las Vegas.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,900
23 million people visit every year for a taste of its intoxicating blend of high stakes,

5
00:00:42,901 --> 00:00:45,760
pop culture, and big entertainment.

6
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:54,340
Here is the world's largest amusement park, a glittering celebration of America's pioneering

7
00:00:54,341 --> 00:01:02,040
personality and the reckless pleasure of putting all your hopes and dreams on one roll of the dice

8
00:01:02,041 --> 00:01:10,540
taking that tumbling ride wherever it leads nearly five billion dollars are wagered here each year

9
00:01:10,541 --> 00:01:18,220
a figure larger than the gross national product of many countries gambling on this scale is more

10
00:01:18,221 --> 00:01:20,660
than just gaming.

11
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:25,160
It's a ceremony, a ritual, a high drama

12
00:01:25,161 --> 00:01:29,560
of winning and losing, of needing to play that next hand.

13
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:34,660
Gilded palaces and exotic temples

14
00:01:34,661 --> 00:01:36,600
rise from the desert floor to create

15
00:01:36,601 --> 00:01:40,480
an astonishing landscape, a suitable setting

16
00:01:40,481 --> 00:01:43,400
for a mythology as colorful as the neon that

17
00:01:43,401 --> 00:01:46,380
illuminates the night air.

18
00:01:46,381 --> 00:01:53,580
Superstars, billionaires, gangsters have all left an indelible impression on this improbable oasis.

19
00:01:55,020 --> 00:02:00,160
Las Vegas will always have that little tinge of, are there truly mob figures still there?

20
00:02:00,161 --> 00:02:06,200
And I think if you took that away, it would hurt us, even though I don't know of any mob figures here.

21
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:11,000
Through a combination of careful design and natural selection,

22
00:02:11,001 --> 00:02:16,840
the city has transformed itself into the world's most spectacular venue for fantasy.

23
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:26,820
Every sound, every image, every aspect persuades people that here, dreams come true.

24
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:32,900
Tonight on Modern Marbles, a diamond in the desert, Las Vegas.

25
00:03:03,069 --> 00:03:05,280
The railroad actually created Las Vegas.

26
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:10,660
Las Vegas was important as a stop on a transportation route

27
00:03:10,661 --> 00:03:14,160
because it had water and because it was strategically located

28
00:03:14,161 --> 00:03:17,460
between Salt Lake and the Pacific Coast.

29
00:03:17,461 --> 00:03:20,800
So in the summer of 1905, basically,

30
00:03:20,801 --> 00:03:24,040
the railroad held an auction right at the head

31
00:03:24,041 --> 00:03:28,380
of present-day Fremont Street and laid out

32
00:03:28,381 --> 00:03:32,240
a very small town site of just 40 square blocks.

33
00:03:32,241 --> 00:03:34,960
And this was to be a sober, industrious, working class

34
00:03:34,961 --> 00:03:37,980
community to support the railroad, very different

35
00:03:37,981 --> 00:03:41,420
than the present image of Las Vegas, obviously.

36
00:03:41,421 --> 00:03:44,580
What they did is limit the sale of alcoholic beverages

37
00:03:44,581 --> 00:03:47,680
to just one or two blocks in town.

38
00:03:47,681 --> 00:03:51,400
But there was an escape clause that a hotel could actually

39
00:03:51,401 --> 00:03:53,660
could also sell intoxicating beverages.

40
00:03:53,661 --> 00:03:56,940
So every little saloon along Fremont Street,

41
00:03:56,941 --> 00:03:58,940
which was the main thoroughfare, then

42
00:03:58,941 --> 00:04:02,480
added a couple of rooms in the back or upstairs.

43
00:04:02,481 --> 00:04:04,360
And from that point, Fremont Street

44
00:04:04,361 --> 00:04:07,840
developed as the saloon district.

45
00:04:07,841 --> 00:04:14,300
Gambling became legal in 1931, but nobody really paid a whole lot of attention to it

46
00:04:14,301 --> 00:04:19,940
because the real news was that they were starting construction of what was then called Boulder Dam.

47
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:26,820
And that was what first caused the population to explode in the late 1920s and early 1930s.

48
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:32,740
Conceived in the late 1920s as a way to control flooding of the Colorado River

49
00:04:32,741 --> 00:04:35,840
and generate power for the western United States,

50
00:04:35,841 --> 00:04:43,640
Boulder Dam was one of the premier public works projects of Franklin Delano Roosevelt's Depression-era administration.

51
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:55,120
Only 30 miles from Las Vegas, the dam attracted thousands of hungry men who needed to be housed and fed on a massive scale.

52
00:04:55,980 --> 00:05:02,200
The scope of the project also attracted more than its share of tourists, eager to see history in the making.

53
00:05:02,201 --> 00:05:07,440
As so many hundreds of thousands of people came to see this huge magnificent project

54
00:05:07,441 --> 00:05:13,240
going up, this was the time when Las Vegas first began to see itself as possibly a resort

55
00:05:13,241 --> 00:05:14,240
destination.

56
00:05:14,241 --> 00:05:23,160
In the name of the people of the United States, to whom you, Boulder Dam, are a symbol of greater

57
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:28,160
things in the future, I call you to life.

58
00:05:28,161 --> 00:05:32,920
At the press of a presidential finger, the opening of Boulder Dam became a symbol of

59
00:05:32,921 --> 00:05:39,640
a new America, digging itself out of economic gloom.

60
00:05:39,641 --> 00:05:42,900
But Las Vegas wasn't celebrating.

61
00:05:42,901 --> 00:05:48,140
But after the dam was completed in 1935, when people began to leave town, and it looked

62
00:05:48,141 --> 00:05:53,920
like that Las Vegas was going to shrink up and become another little railroad town again.

63
00:05:53,921 --> 00:05:56,848
But as World War II approached, the U.S.

64
00:05:56,849 --> 00:06:00,800
Army Air Force decided that this was an ideal location

65
00:06:00,801 --> 00:06:06,360
because of the weather and inland location to build an air base, which started in June

66
00:06:06,361 --> 00:06:13,680
of 1941.

67
00:06:13,681 --> 00:06:18,400
The nation would be shocked by the surprise attack on Pearl Harbor in December.

68
00:06:18,401 --> 00:06:23,180
But the military had already begun to prepare for war six months earlier with an air base

69
00:06:23,181 --> 00:06:26,220
and magnesium plant near Las Vegas.

70
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:30,720
It gave the area a solid economic base.

71
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:34,520
Las Vegas' population in 1940 was just 8,000 people.

72
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:38,220
By the summer of 1942, that population figure in Southern

73
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,000
Nevada had basically tripled.

74
00:06:40,100 --> 00:06:43,760
People were literally brought from all corners of America

75
00:06:43,860 --> 00:06:46,900
to come to work in the defense establishment.

76
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,980
Las Vegas came to be known as a playtown during those years,

77
00:06:49,981 --> 00:06:53,020
even though tourism was not big yet.

78
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:57,520
It was really the end of the war in 1945

79
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:01,220
when millions of servicemen are demobilized,

80
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:03,460
gas rationing is off,

81
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:06,700
after a point building materials are available,

82
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:08,940
and then somebody starts to build a hotel

83
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:13,300
way out on the Los Angeles highway called the Flamingo Hotel.

84
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:16,100
A Californian by the name of Billy Wilkerson

85
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:19,680
had started to build a hotel called the Flamingo.

86
00:07:19,681 --> 00:07:22,880
During the early phases of construction, however, he ran out of money.

87
00:07:23,960 --> 00:07:27,660
He was put into contact with Benjamin Bugsy Siegel.

88
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:32,620
Siegel eventually took over the project.

89
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:39,300
But contrary to popular belief, the Flamingo Hotel was not the dream and vision of Bugsy Siegel.

90
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:42,620
It was the dream and vision of Billy Wilkerson.

91
00:07:44,140 --> 00:07:46,000
It was an elegant establishment.

92
00:07:46,001 --> 00:07:50,640
It wasn't a sawdust joint like some of the casinos were referred to downtown.

93
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:53,900
Bugsy could afford to build a swank joint.

94
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:59,960
After all, he had the financial backing of Meyer Lansky and the boys from Murder Incorporated,

95
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:05,260
the homicidal mob that had the muscle and money to get their way.

96
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:11,600
I met Bugsy Siegel in Hollywood a long time ago, and I believe it was Virginia Hill.

97
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,700
He was going with a lady called Virginia Hill, or living with her, whatever,

98
00:08:15,701 --> 00:08:17,340
In those days, we didn't talk about that.

99
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:18,940
Virginia Hill didn't either,

100
00:08:19,640 --> 00:08:21,680
even when grilled by Senate investigators

101
00:08:21,681 --> 00:08:24,540
about her ties to Bugsy and the mob.

102
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:28,620
I never got money from any of those fellas.

103
00:08:28,621 --> 00:08:30,080
None of those fellas.

104
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:32,480
None of these that I've been reading about

105
00:08:32,481 --> 00:08:34,520
or none that I knew, they never gave me anything.

106
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:36,080
Do you still bet the horse races?

107
00:08:36,081 --> 00:08:37,160
That's the only thing I...

108
00:08:37,161 --> 00:08:39,580
Now? I wouldn't... I don't bet anything.

109
00:08:39,700 --> 00:08:40,900
Now I'm afraid I'll win,

110
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:43,160
and then they'll say I made more money than I did.

111
00:08:43,161 --> 00:08:46,900
Bugsy had a way of putting ladies at ease.

112
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:49,080
He couldn't have been friendlier.

113
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:50,300
He couldn't have been more charming.

114
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:51,520
He loved to show people.

115
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:52,520
He liked a good time.

116
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:54,420
I didn't know the bad side.

117
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:58,060
But Bugsy had a very bad side.

118
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:04,020
And fearing retaliation, he turned his private suite at the Flamingo into a personal fortress.

119
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:08,300
Bulletproof glass was installed to shield his bedroom from gunfire.

120
00:09:08,860 --> 00:09:12,880
Three-foot-thick walls could protect him from a possible assassin's bomb.

121
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:18,000
and a hidden escape tunnel offered quick retreat in case of emergency.

122
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:21,180
He had a right to be scared.

123
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:25,880
He had gambled too much of the mob's money on building the hotel,

124
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:28,920
and they wanted the debt settled in blood.

125
00:09:30,260 --> 00:09:33,600
The account was paid in full when Bugsy was ambushed

126
00:09:33,601 --> 00:09:36,500
during a trip to his Los Angeles home in 1947.

127
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:41,860
He created an oasis in the desert.

128
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:48,160
That made it unique and special unto itself.

129
00:09:48,161 --> 00:09:54,160
But its real success came because he did it and he was dead.

130
00:09:54,161 --> 00:10:00,160
Fortunately, subsequent owners haven't had to die to have their places be successful.

131
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:08,160
Nevada is different than a lot of states because we're isolated.

132
00:10:08,161 --> 00:10:11,160
And that's the only reason that gaming was able to develop like it did here,

133
00:10:11,161 --> 00:10:17,720
here is because we were isolated you had to drive or fly you had to have the idea when you came here

134
00:10:17,721 --> 00:10:24,600
that you're going to go to las vegas in the decade that followed world war ii the idea of las vegas

135
00:10:24,601 --> 00:10:31,240
attracted high rollers and dreamers in ever-increasing numbers as hotels began to compete

136
00:10:31,241 --> 00:10:37,880
for the resort trade the town stretched south along a flat ribbon which became known as the strip

137
00:10:37,881 --> 00:10:42,880
Here was room to grow and gamble.

138
00:10:42,881 --> 00:10:49,120
There was a time when I first came here where the three S's were the buildings, the Sands,

139
00:10:49,121 --> 00:10:50,620
the Sahara and the Stardust.

140
00:10:50,621 --> 00:10:54,160
If you could stay there, you were at the capital of the world.

141
00:10:54,161 --> 00:10:56,200
They were semi-themed.

142
00:10:56,201 --> 00:11:02,400
Not really, it was more with words and cocktail napkins and matches than it was with architecture.

143
00:11:02,401 --> 00:11:06,780
Then along came Caesar's Palace and those buildings began to pale.

144
00:11:06,781 --> 00:11:15,300
For a town based on the dream of winning a fortune, creating a grand fantasy became a crucial competitive edge.

145
00:11:18,180 --> 00:11:24,040
In 1966, Caesar's Palace introduced a whole new way of looking at gambling resorts.

146
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:35,180
Caesar's is an elaborate monument to fantasy, a trip to a hedonistic time and place that is at the core of that peculiar Las Vegas experience.

147
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:39,900
Even though it was actually arabesque in its look,

148
00:11:40,420 --> 00:11:43,460
they absolutely went to Rome,

149
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:46,480
or they brought you to Rome when you entered the place.

150
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,380
I thought that was terrific.

151
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:52,580
Caesar's Palace set a new standard.

152
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:57,540
It cost nearly $30 million to build,

153
00:11:58,200 --> 00:12:01,000
and that was an astounding figure in its day.

154
00:12:01,001 --> 00:12:09,000
In fact, that was nearly five times the amount Bugsy Siegel had spent on the original Flamingo.

155
00:12:09,001 --> 00:12:15,000
The Caesars was perfect. It was the ideal casino.

156
00:12:15,001 --> 00:12:18,000
It had all of the elements in the right place.

157
00:12:18,001 --> 00:12:25,000
The casino was in the center, and then right around it were the restaurants and the showroom and the bars.

158
00:12:25,001 --> 00:12:28,280
You got good entertainment, wonderful entertainment.

159
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:30,920
You got great food at a very reasonable price,

160
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,920
but the whole idea was to get you into the casino

161
00:12:32,921 --> 00:12:35,000
because you had to go through the casino.

162
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:37,560
You could go around it, but basically through the casino

163
00:12:37,561 --> 00:12:40,220
to get to those rooms, and then there was the pool.

164
00:12:40,980 --> 00:12:43,340
And all of those were just perfect.

165
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:47,700
Caesar's also demonstrated a basic truth.

166
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:50,020
People love spectacle.

167
00:12:50,940 --> 00:12:53,360
And here was a modern eye-popping twist

168
00:12:53,361 --> 00:13:00,480
on the ancient roman theme when evil knievel drove his motorcycle over the elaborate fountain in front

169
00:13:00,481 --> 00:13:08,240
of the hotel las vegas was catapulted into a new era we now know that people will come and they

170
00:13:08,241 --> 00:13:13,360
want to give us the money and they want to give it to us their way they'll find the casino that

171
00:13:13,361 --> 00:13:19,040
isn't what's important what's important is they go away with a great experience creating that great

172
00:13:19,041 --> 00:13:26,040
Great experience is now the challenge of architects and designers who bring these improbable fantasies to life.

173
00:13:26,041 --> 00:13:28,190
We're not talking about great architecture.

174
00:13:28,191 --> 00:13:31,040
We're talking about architecture that satisfies the need.

175
00:13:31,041 --> 00:13:36,040
You go downtown, it stands out. That's what's really important.

176
00:13:36,041 --> 00:13:40,040
You serve the people so that they remember the whole experience, not just the building,

177
00:13:40,041 --> 00:13:48,040
but they remember the food, the way they were treated, the guest room.

178
00:13:48,041 --> 00:13:51,440
Hell, the bathrooms down there are drop dead.

179
00:13:51,441 --> 00:13:54,380
Just to go into a public toilet room is an experience.

180
00:13:55,780 --> 00:13:56,620
That's important.

181
00:13:57,640 --> 00:13:59,260
Make a promise out front,

182
00:14:00,260 --> 00:14:02,920
and then keep the promise all the way through.

183
00:14:02,921 --> 00:14:05,080
So that when people are in your space,

184
00:14:05,081 --> 00:14:07,500
they never have a moment of disappointment.

185
00:14:10,420 --> 00:14:12,300
The city's inexhaustible party

186
00:14:12,301 --> 00:14:15,140
has always attracted a gaming crowd.

187
00:14:15,141 --> 00:14:20,540
But when Circus Circus presented trapeze artists flying over the tables to entertain children

188
00:14:20,541 --> 00:14:26,260
while parents gambled, it was obvious that a whole new audience was being primed for

189
00:14:26,261 --> 00:14:27,620
the Las Vegas experience.

190
00:14:28,760 --> 00:14:33,380
Over the last two decades, Vegas has added over 86,000 rooms.

191
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:38,640
This phenomenal growth is due, in part, to successfully targeting the family market.

192
00:14:38,641 --> 00:14:46,980
Instead of slick sophistication or scintillating come-ons, the temptation of Las Vegas now

193
00:14:46,981 --> 00:14:53,520
crosses all boundaries of age and culture.

194
00:14:53,521 --> 00:14:56,360
Smoky casinos used to dominate the landscape.

195
00:14:56,361 --> 00:15:04,100
Today, resorts feature a dolphin habitat, albino tigers, water slides, and high-tech

196
00:15:04,101 --> 00:15:12,380
arcades to guarantee an entertainment jackpot. The MGM Grand, the largest hotel

197
00:15:12,381 --> 00:15:18,420
in the world, is a case in point. Here the magic of Hollywood lures entire

198
00:15:18,421 --> 00:15:26,600
families. The appeal is so great that the hotel features an astounding 5,005

199
00:15:26,601 --> 00:15:35,720
rooms serviced by 97 elevators. Combined with the mammoth Excalibur and Tropicana

200
00:15:35,721 --> 00:15:40,880
resorts across the street, this single Las Vegas intersection boasts more hotel

201
00:15:40,881 --> 00:15:45,920
rooms than the entire city of San Francisco. Every time you build a new

202
00:15:45,921 --> 00:15:52,000
room in this city you also have now one more than somebody else has and usually

203
00:15:52,001 --> 00:15:54,380
probably the best in the world.

204
00:15:54,980 --> 00:15:57,720
It has changed since when I first came to this state.

205
00:15:58,180 --> 00:15:59,460
They're bringing more families in.

206
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:01,400
We have more family entertainment.

207
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:04,240
It's fun for me because I have a clean act.

208
00:16:04,740 --> 00:16:06,580
And people like to see a clean act now,

209
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:08,420
whereas they used to come to Las Vegas and,

210
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:11,000
oh, we can hear things that, blue humor that you don't hear.

211
00:16:11,080 --> 00:16:12,480
But now they have the blue humor on TV,

212
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:14,400
so they come to Las Vegas to hear clean acts.

213
00:16:16,020 --> 00:16:20,940
I believe that Las Vegas has finally come to where I'm at.

214
00:16:20,941 --> 00:16:29,240
I always feel like that the crowd should not be challenged on a religious basis.

215
00:16:29,620 --> 00:16:32,180
They should not be challenged on a political basis.

216
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:34,300
They are there to be entertained.

217
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:37,180
We're not there to teach them, we're not there to change their religion,

218
00:16:37,320 --> 00:16:39,360
and we're not there to change their political beliefs.

219
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:40,840
We're there to entertain them.

220
00:16:41,740 --> 00:16:44,280
The things that I believe in about entertaining

221
00:16:44,281 --> 00:16:49,820
and what we are about as performers, Vegas has gotten to.

222
00:16:49,821 --> 00:16:56,800
I don't like to do anything here that I would be afraid to do anywhere in the country or anywhere in the world.

223
00:16:58,340 --> 00:17:06,960
As the competition to create family attractions heated up, Las Vegas entrepreneurs characteristically raised the stakes.

224
00:17:09,300 --> 00:17:14,820
In the early 1900s, architect Joel Bergman was invited to participate in the design of the most explosive

225
00:17:14,821 --> 00:17:20,200
explosive entertainment gambling resort ever attempted in Las Vegas.

226
00:17:20,760 --> 00:17:24,720
We had stumbled around for a couple of months with various kinds of motifs.

227
00:17:25,040 --> 00:17:26,720
We knew we were going to do a theme place.

228
00:17:27,200 --> 00:17:28,720
We knew it had to be powerful.

229
00:17:29,380 --> 00:17:32,600
The result was called Treasure Island,

230
00:17:32,940 --> 00:17:39,220
and it explored new territory by making gambling secondary to theatrical presentation.

231
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:43,280
There's a free show out front.

232
00:17:43,281 --> 00:17:46,280
At Treasure Island, we made the entrance part of the show.

233
00:17:46,281 --> 00:17:49,780
And there are 3,000 to 5,000 people every hour and a half

234
00:17:49,781 --> 00:17:51,280
that line up for that show.

235
00:17:51,281 --> 00:17:53,280
Pass this onto his royal highness!

236
00:17:53,281 --> 00:17:54,280
And it's super.

237
00:17:54,281 --> 00:17:57,280
And most of them don't go inside the casino after they've seen it.

238
00:17:57,281 --> 00:17:59,280
It doesn't matter.

239
00:17:59,281 --> 00:18:02,280
We've created activity, we've brought people together,

240
00:18:02,281 --> 00:18:05,280
and everybody goes away with a wonderful feeling.

241
00:18:05,281 --> 00:18:08,280
If pirates really knew how to spend their loot,

242
00:18:08,281 --> 00:18:10,280
this is what they'd have done with it.

243
00:18:33,303 --> 00:18:39,200
Shortly after World War II, the federal government appropriated hundreds of square miles of southern Nevada

244
00:18:39,201 --> 00:18:45,000
for what was to be one of the most ambitious and expensive scientific projects in history.

245
00:18:48,200 --> 00:18:54,160
In January of 1951, a B-29 bomber released the first atomic bomb

246
00:18:54,161 --> 00:18:57,200
to be dropped over the Las Vegas proving grounds.

247
00:18:59,640 --> 00:19:05,540
Above-ground nuclear testing continued throughout the 1950s and into the 1960s.

248
00:19:05,541 --> 00:19:12,960
What is, I think, interesting about that, apart from the financial consequences of it,

249
00:19:13,100 --> 00:19:16,700
because it brought a lot of money into Las Vegas,

250
00:19:17,160 --> 00:19:21,820
but Las Vegas also learned how to use the nuclear bomb for publicity.

251
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:27,440
And they would have things like Miss Atomic Bomb Contests.

252
00:19:28,100 --> 00:19:31,500
The city fancied itself in the vanguard of the 20th century,

253
00:19:31,501 --> 00:19:38,640
the atomic age when America wielded intoxicating new power well they did

254
00:19:38,641 --> 00:19:45,500
have atomic cocktails equal parts of vodka brandy and champagne with a touch

255
00:19:45,501 --> 00:19:51,420
of sherry that was the winning recipe for an atomic cocktail Nevada's nuclear

256
00:19:51,421 --> 00:19:56,260
weapons testing attracted even more visitors to Las Vegas they were lured by

257
00:19:56,261 --> 00:20:00,300
the promise of another great entertainment extravaganza a show

258
00:20:00,301 --> 00:20:04,740
guaranteed to blow them away.

259
00:20:04,741 --> 00:20:08,700
Even the hotels on the then emerging Las Vegas Strip

260
00:20:08,701 --> 00:20:12,080
would publish schedules of nuclear tests,

261
00:20:12,081 --> 00:20:15,180
would invite their guests to go with them,

262
00:20:15,181 --> 00:20:17,820
take box lunches up to the mountains.

263
00:20:17,821 --> 00:20:21,620
And in the pre-dawn hours, they would be able to see the sky

264
00:20:21,621 --> 00:20:25,300
light up and those pretty mushroom clouds float away.

265
00:20:25,301 --> 00:20:30,300
So Las Vegas actually learned to profit from the bomb.

266
00:20:30,301 --> 00:20:33,460
But there were serious consequences to Vegas' new show.

267
00:20:39,600 --> 00:20:41,740
The test site was designed theoretically

268
00:20:41,741 --> 00:20:44,740
so the prevailing winds would carry those mushroom clouds

269
00:20:44,741 --> 00:20:48,160
up to the northeast away from the major population

270
00:20:48,161 --> 00:20:49,920
center in Las Vegas.

271
00:20:49,921 --> 00:20:52,680
People in Lincoln County and in southwestern Utah,

272
00:20:52,681 --> 00:20:56,500
of course, subsequently developed unusually high rates

273
00:20:56,501 --> 00:20:57,540
of cancer.

274
00:20:57,541 --> 00:21:00,260
So there's a very serious side to the nuclear testing.

275
00:21:00,780 --> 00:21:03,860
The hundreds of downwinders, men, women, and children

276
00:21:03,861 --> 00:21:07,240
who unwittingly received a fatal dose of radioactivity,

277
00:21:08,240 --> 00:21:11,780
discovered the hard way that the odds in life aren't always fair.

278
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:17,320
We all have a lot to win, but even more to lose.

279
00:21:17,321 --> 00:21:31,140
But despite that as they do despite everything in Las Vegas the games go on

280
00:21:47,320 --> 00:21:51,320
People are a lot like bugs. They're attracted to light.

281
00:21:51,321 --> 00:21:57,320
So when you have a nice tight concentration of light, people are attracted to that.

282
00:21:57,321 --> 00:22:04,320
Then it's a game of who can do the splashiest, the brightest sign to attract people to their location.

283
00:22:06,320 --> 00:22:09,320
Las Vegas is truly an electric city.

